% ============================================================================
% chamber-branch — DERIVED, not looked up: which BRANCH of the U.S. federal
% government a named CHAMBER of Congress belongs to (adj-facts-stdlib,
% CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "Is the Senate part of the legislative branch?" is a question no shipped
% civics table answers on its own, and — importantly — it is a question no
% sentence on the cited source answers directly either. USA.gov's
% "Branches of the U.S. government" page never writes "the Senate is part
% of the legislative branch". What it writes is two SEPARATE facts, one
% level apart:
%
%     "The legislative branch is made up of Congress:"   -> the branch's member is Congress
%     "The U.S. Senate" (nested under that stem)         -> the Senate is a chamber OF Congress
%
% This file is the FIRST `rule` in the `civics/` domain, and it derives the
% answer by composing those two already-shipped, already-grounded tables
% rather than asserting a third row nobody could trace back to a sentence:
%
%     ? chamber_branch(senate, $B)
%       % legislative
%
% The derivation is a two-hop join through the `congress` atom that both
% sibling libraries independently bind:
%
%     congress_chamber(senate, congress)                 [congress-chamber.adj]
%     government_branch_member(legislative, congress)    [government-branch-member.adj]
%     -----------------------------------------------------------------------
%     chamber_branch(senate, legislative)                [derived here]
%
% Every answer therefore carries BOTH premises' citations in its `steps`
% trail — the provenance of the conclusion is the composition of the
% provenance of its premises, which is exactly the property that makes a
% derived answer auditable rather than merely plausible. This is the shape
% `biology/abo-genotype-antigen.adj` already established for heredity,
% applied here to the civics graph.
%
% WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT WAY TO ANSWER IT. `government-branch-member.adj`
% deliberately ABSTAINS on `senate`/`house_of_representatives`, because the
% source nests them under Congress rather than naming them as direct
% members of the branch; `congress-chamber.adj` was then split out as its
% own predicate specifically so the nesting the source states would not be
% flattened away. Both of those decisions were made so that this rule
% could exist. Deriving the fact here — instead of quietly adding
% `government_branch_member(legislative, senate)` back as a row — keeps the
% source's own two-level structure intact AND still answers the learner's
% question. The abstention was never a gap to be papered over; it was the
% seam the derivation joins on.
%
% The rule also runs BACKWARD, which enumerates the chambers of a branch:
%
%     ? chamber_branch($C, legislative)
%       % senate
%       % house_of_representatives
%
% ABSTENTION IS INHERITED, NOT RESTATED. This rule adds no facts of its
% own, so it abstains exactly where its premises do. `supreme_court` and
% `president` are named institutions of the other two branches, and
% `congress_chamber` abstains on both (they are not chambers of Congress);
% the derivation therefore finds no premise to join and abstains too,
% rather than inventing a chamber-of relationship. The same holds for the
% open-ended category phrases both sibling tables exclude.
%
% Provenance: the `source` below is the branch-membership stem that
% grounds the SECOND hop; the first hop's grounding (the chamber sub-list)
% is carried by `congress-chamber.adj` itself and rides along in the proof
% trail. Both were curl-fetched and read byte-for-byte from the raw markup
% when those libraries were written. `trust authoritative` — USA.gov is
% the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to government
% information, the same tier and the SAME page both premises cite.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "congress-chamber.adj"
import "government-branch-member.adj"

rule { head: chamber_branch($Chamber, $Branch)
       when: congress_chamber($Chamber, $Body), government_branch_member($Branch, $Body)
       source "The legislative branch is made up of Congress:"
       locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
       trust authoritative }
